{"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/masum1834e/ai-engineering?scriptVersionId=371799048\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"5c31ba86","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010845,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:12:55.969797+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:12:55.958952+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Prompt Engineering\n","\n","**Prompt engineering is the process of designing, structuring, and optimizing instructions or inputs given to a Large Language Model (LLM) to obtain accurate, relevant, consistent, and useful outputs.**\n","\n","In simple terms:\n","\n","You give instructions to an LLM, and the LLM generates a response based on those instructions and its learned patterns.\n","\n","The quality of your instructions can significantly influence the quality of the output.\n","\n","**Example: Without prompt engineering**\n","\n","User:\n","\n","```\n","Tell me about Python.\n","```\n","\n","Possible LLM response:\n","\n","- Python is a programming language.\n","- It is easy to learn.\n","- It is used in AI, web development, and automation.\n","\n","This response is correct, but generic.\n","\n","**Example: With prompt engineering**\n","\n","```\n","You are a senior Python developer and technical instructor.\n","\n","Explain Python to someone who has experience\n","with JavaScript but is new to Python.\n","\n","Structure your response into:\n","\n","1. What Python is\n","2. Important features\n","3. Python vs JavaScript\n","4. Practical code examples\n","5. Real-world applications\n","\n","Use simple language and practical examples.\n","```\n","\n","Now the model has:\n","\n","- A defined role.\n","- A specific audience.\n","- A clear objective.\n","- A structured output requirement.\n","- A communication style.\n","\n","This makes the response more targeted.\n","\n","**Important:** Prompt engineering does not change the LLM's underlying weights or retrain the model. It changes the input context and instructions used during inference.\n","\n","## Inside Prompt Engineering\n","\n","Before learning prompt techniques, understand what happens when you send a prompt to an LLM.\n","\n","Consider this prompt:\n","\n","```\n","What is machine learning?\n","```\n","\n","The process generally looks like this:\n","\n","1. **User submits a prompt**: `What is machine learning?`\n","2. **Tokenization**: The text is divided into tokens (subword units, words, or punctuation).\n","3. **Token embedding**: Tokens are converted into numerical vector representations.\n","4. **Transformer processing**: Attention mechanisms and neural network layers process the input context.\n","5. **Next-token prediction**: The model calculates probabilities for possible next tokens.\n","6. **Sampling or decoding**: A decoding strategy selects the next token.\n","7. **Output generation**: The model continues generating tokens until it reaches a stopping condition.\n","\n","For example, the model might assign these illustrative probabilities to the next token:\n","\n","| Possible token | Probability |\n","| -------------- | ----------- |\n","| Machine        | 45%         |\n","| Learning       | 25%         |\n","| Artificial     | 20%         |\n","| Computer       | 10%         |\n","\n","The model selects a token according to its decoding configuration and continues generating.\n","\n","Prompt engineering influences the context from which these predictions are made.\n","\n","It does not guarantee that the model will always select the correct answer.\n","\n","## Anatomy of a Good Prompt\n","\n","A well-designed prompt often contains several components.\n","\n","I recommend understanding this structure:\n","\n","### Prompt structure\n","\n","**Role / Persona**\n","\n","Define who the LLM should act as.\n","\n","Example: You are a senior AI engineer.\n","\n","**Task / Instruction**\n","\n","Define what the LLM should accomplish.\n","\n","Example: Explain how RAG works.\n","\n","**Context**\n","\n","Provide relevant background information.\n","\n","Example: The audience knows Python but is new to RAG.\n","\n","**Constraints**\n","\n","Define limitations and requirements.\n","\n","Example: Avoid unnecessary mathematical details.\n","\n","**Output Format**\n","\n","Define the expected response structure.\n","\n","Example: Return Markdown with headings and Python examples.\n","\n","### Examples (Optional)\n","\n","Show examples of desired inputs and outputs.\n","\n","Example: Provide a sample RAG pipeline.\n","\n","Let's combine these components.\n","\n","```\n","ROLE:\n","You are a senior AI engineer specializing in\n","LLM application development.\n","\n","TASK:\n","Explain Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).\n","\n","CONTEXT:\n","The learner understands Python, REST APIs,\n","LLMs, and vector databases.\n","\n","REQUIREMENTS:\n","1. Explain the concept step by step.\n","2. Explain why RAG is needed.\n","3. Describe the complete architecture.\n","4. Include practical Python examples.\n","5. Explain common implementation problems.\n","6. Discuss production-level best practices.\n","\n","CONSTRAINTS:\n","- Use technically accurate explanations.\n","- Avoid unnecessary theoretical complexity.\n","- Do not invent information.\n","- Explicitly identify uncertainty.\n","\n","OUTPUT FORMAT:\n","1. Definition\n","2. Architecture\n","3. Working mechanism\n","4. Implementation\n","5. Real-world use cases\n","6. Interview questions\n","```\n","\n","This is a structured prompt.\n","\n","However, not every prompt needs all six components. Simple questions should have simple prompts.\n","\n","## Types of Prompt Engineering Techniques\n","\n","This is one of the most important areas.\n","\n","There are several established prompting techniques. Each technique solves a different problem.\n","\n","### Zero-Shot Prompting\n","\n","Zero-shot prompting means asking an LLM to perform a task without providing any examples.\n","\n","You simply provide an instruction.\n","\n","**Example:**\n","\n","```\n","Classify the following customer review\n","as Positive, Negative, or Neutral.\n","\n","Review:\n","\"The product quality is excellent,\n","but delivery was very slow.\"\n","\n","Return only the classification.\n","```\n","\n","Possible output:\n","\n","```\n","Neutral\n","```\n","\n","The LLM uses its existing training and learned capabilities to perform the task.\n","\n","**When to use it:**\n","\n","- Simple question answering.\n","- Text classification.\n","- Summarization.\n","- Translation.\n","- Basic information extraction.\n","- Tasks where the expected output is straightforward.\n","\n","**Advantages:**\n","\n","- Simple to implement.\n","- Requires fewer tokens.\n","- No example dataset is necessary.\n","- Often sufficient for common tasks.\n","\n","**Limitations:**\n","\n","- Output may be inconsistent.\n","- Complex instructions may be misunderstood.\n","- The model may not follow a specialized output pattern.\n","\n","### One-Shot Prompting\n","\n","One-shot prompting means providing one example of the desired input-output behavior.\n","\n","The example teaches the model how you expect it to perform the task.\n","\n","**Example:**\n","\n","```\n","Classify customer reviews as Positive or Negative.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","Review: \"The product is amazing.\"\n","Classification: Positive\n","\n","Now classify:\n","\n","Review: \"The application crashes frequently.\"\n","Classification:\n","```\n","\n","Possible output:\n","\n","```\n","Negative\n","```\n","\n","The model uses the example as a reference for the next task.\n","\n","**When to use it:**\n","\n","- When you need a particular output style.\n","- When the task has a specialized format.\n","- When zero-shot results are inconsistent.\n","\n","### Few-Shot Prompting\n","\n","Few-shot prompting means providing multiple examples to demonstrate the desired behavior.\n","\n","It is especially useful when the task requires specific patterns, classification rules, or output formats.\n","\n","**Example:**\n","\n","```\n","Classify customer feedback.\n","\n","Example 1:\n","Input: \"Excellent service and friendly staff.\"\n","Output: Positive\n","\n","Example 2:\n","Input: \"Terrible experience.\"\n","Output: Negative\n","\n","Example 3:\n","Input: \"The service was okay.\"\n","Output: Neutral\n","\n","Example 4:\n","Input: \"Very disappointed with the quality.\"\n","Output: Negative\n","\n","Now classify:\n","\n","Input: \"I really enjoyed using this application.\"\n","Output:\n","```\n","\n","Possible output:\n","\n","```\n","Positive\n","```\n","\n","**How does few-shot prompting work?**\n","\n","Suppose you are building a customer support AI system.\n","\n","You want the LLM to categorize customer complaints into:\n","\n","- Payment issue\n","- Technical issue\n","- Account issue\n","- Delivery issue\n","\n","Instead of explaining every classification rule in detail, you provide several representative examples.\n","\n","The model recognizes the patterns demonstrated in the examples and applies them to new inputs.\n","\n","**Important interview point:** Few-shot prompting is a form of in-context learning. It does not update the model's parameters or permanently train the model.\n","\n","**Comparison**\n","\n","| Technique | Examples provided |\n","| --------- | ----------------- |\n","| Zero-shot | 0                 |\n","| One-shot  | 1                 |\n","| Few-shot  | 2 or more         |\n","\n","### Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting\n","\n","Chain-of-Thought prompting encourages an LLM to work through intermediate reasoning steps before producing an answer.\n","\n","It is useful for tasks involving:\n","\n","- Mathematics.\n","- Logical reasoning.\n","- Multi-step problem solving.\n","- Complex analysis.\n","- Planning.\n","\n","**Without CoT**\n","\n","```\n","A product costs $100.\n","It has a 20% discount.\n","\n","What is the final price?\n","```\n","\n","Output:\n","\n","```\n","$80\n","```\n","\n","**With CoT-style prompting**\n","\n","```\n","Solve this problem carefully.\n","\n","A product costs $100.\n","It has a 20% discount.\n","\n","Calculate:\n","1. The discount amount.\n","2. The final price after discount.\n","\n","Provide the calculation and final answer.\n","```\n","\n","Output:\n","\n","```\n","Original price = $100\n","\n","Discount = 100 × 20 / 100\n","         = $20\n","\n","Final price = 100 - 20\n","            = $80\n","```\n","\n","**Why does CoT help?**\n","\n","Some problems require multiple intermediate operations.\n","\n","Breaking a problem into steps can make it easier for the model to handle those operations.\n","\n","However, requesting a detailed explanation does not guarantee that the explanation faithfully represents the model's internal reasoning.\n","\n","**Advanced consideration**\n","\n","Modern reasoning models may perform internal reasoning without needing an explicit instruction such as \"think step by step.\"\n","\n","In production, you should evaluate answer correctness rather than assume that a longer reasoning explanation means better reasoning.\n","\n","### Self-Consistency Prompting\n","\n","Self-consistency is an extension of reasoning-based prompting.\n","\n","The basic idea:\n","\n","1. Generate multiple candidate solutions.\n","2. Compare their final answers.\n","3. Select the most consistent answer.\n","\n","Suppose an LLM solves a mathematical problem three times.\n","\n","| Attempt | Answer |\n","| ------- | ------ |\n","| 1       | 120    |\n","| 2       | 120    |\n","| 3       | 100    |\n","\n","The majority answer is 120.\n","\n","![Self Consistence Prompting](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/self_consistence_prompting.png)\n","\n","**Practical example**\n","\n","```python\n","from collections import Counter\n","\n","answers = [\"120\", \"120\", \"100\", \"120\", \"100\"]\n","\n","most_common = Counter(answers).most_common(1)\n","\n","print(most_common[0][0])\n","```\n","\n","Output:\n","\n","```\n","120\n","```\n","\n","**When to use:**\n","\n","- Mathematical reasoning.\n","- Complex question answering.\n","- Tasks where multiple independent candidate solutions are useful.\n","\n","**Limitation:** Multiple generations increase cost and latency. Majority voting can also select a wrong answer if the candidates share the same systematic error.\n","\n","### Role-Based Prompting\n","\n","Role-based prompting means assigning a specific role or persona to the LLM.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","You are a senior software architect\n","with 15 years of experience in\n","distributed systems.\n","\n","Review the following architecture\n","and identify potential scalability problems.\n","```\n","\n","Another example:\n","\n","```\n","You are an IELTS examiner.\n","\n","Evaluate my speaking answer based on:\n","1. Fluency and coherence\n","2. Lexical resource\n","3. Grammatical range and accuracy\n","4. Pronunciation\n","\n","Provide constructive feedback.\n","```\n","\n","**Why is it useful?**\n","\n","A role provides behavioral context.\n","\n","It helps guide:\n","\n","- Tone.\n","- Vocabulary.\n","- Level of detail.\n","- Perspective.\n","- Domain-specific framing.\n","\n","**Important:** Assigning a role does not give the LLM actual professional credentials or guarantee expert-level correctness.\n","\n","### Contextual Prompting\n","\n","Contextual prompting means supplying relevant background information before asking the model to perform a task.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","Context:\n","\n","We are developing an e-commerce platform.\n","\n","Our backend uses:\n","- FastAPI\n","- MongoDB\n","- Redis\n","- React\n","\n","Task:\n","\n","Design an architecture for an AI-powered\n","product recommendation system.\n","\n","Consider our existing technology stack.\n","```\n","\n","Without context, the LLM may suggest technologies that are unnecessary or incompatible with your application.\n","\n","With context, it can produce more relevant recommendations.\n","\n","Context can include:\n","\n","- User information.\n","- Business rules.\n","- Documents.\n","- Conversation history.\n","- Database schemas.\n","- API specifications.\n","- Retrieved knowledge.\n","\n","This technique is particularly important in RAG systems and AI agents.\n","\n","### Instruction-Based Prompting\n","\n","Instruction-based prompting focuses on giving clear, direct, unambiguous instructions.\n","\n","Weak prompt:\n","\n","```\n","Analyze this data.\n","```\n","\n","Better prompt:\n","\n","```\n","Analyze the following sales data.\n","\n","Calculate:\n","1. Total revenue.\n","2. Average order value.\n","3. Best-performing product.\n","4. Monthly revenue growth.\n","\n","Use the supplied data only.\n","\n","Return the results in a Markdown table.\n","```\n","\n","The difference is task specificity.\n","\n","### Constraint-Based Prompting\n","\n","Constraint-based prompting tells the model what it must or must not do.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","Generate a product description.\n","\n","Constraints:\n","- Maximum 100 words.\n","- Use simple English.\n","- Do not mention competitors.\n","- Do not invent product features.\n","- Include three key benefits.\n","```\n","\n","Constraints can control:\n","\n","- Output length.\n","- Language.\n","- Tone.\n","- Allowed information.\n","- Response structure.\n","- Business rules.\n","- Safety requirements.\n","\n","Constraints are useful when developing production AI applications because the output often needs to comply with business requirements.\n","\n","However, prompt constraints alone are not security guarantees. Important restrictions should also be enforced in application code."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"e2737f2b","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:12:55.989052Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:12:55.9886Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.146198Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.145111Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.170314,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.148692+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:12:55.978378+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["from kaggle_secrets import UserSecretsClient\n","\n","# Load API key from Kaggle Secrets\n","secrets = UserSecretsClient()\n","GROQ_API_KEY = secrets.get_secret(\"GROQ_API_KEY\")"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":2,"id":"87c578f4","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.174277Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.173418Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.862013Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.860783Z"},"papermill":{"duration":6.70599,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.864063+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:12:56.158073+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"]}],"source":["%pip install -q langchain-groq"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":3,"id":"8990d759","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.883313Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.882471Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.522939Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.521907Z"},"papermill":{"duration":2.652842,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.52517+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:02.872328+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["from langchain_groq import ChatGroq\n","\n","llm = ChatGroq(\n","    model=\"qwen/qwen3.8-27b\",\n","    temperature=0,\n","    api_key=GROQ_API_KEY,\n","    max_tokens=200\n",")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"f32c1527","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008318,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.541899+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.533581+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Sampling Strategies\n","\n","When an LLM generates text, it does not directly choose a sentence. Instead, it predicts the probability of the next token.\n","\n","For example, suppose the prompt is:\n","\n","> The cat is sitting on the ...\n","\n","The model might produce the following probability distribution:\n","\n","**Next-token probability distribution**\n","\n","- mat: `70%`\n","- floor: `20%`\n","- roof: `7%`\n","- table: `3%`\n","\n","The model has assigned probabilities to possible next tokens.\n","\n","But how does it select the actual token?\n","\n","That is where sampling strategies come in.\n","\n","**Sampling is the process of selecting a token from the probability distribution predicted by the model.**\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Greedy decoding always chooses `mat`.\n","- Random sampling might choose `floor`.\n","- Top-k sampling might choose among the most likely k tokens.\n","- Top-p sampling might choose from a dynamic group of tokens.\n","- Temperature changes how sharp or flat the probability distribution is.\n","\n","The choice of sampling strategy affects the creativity, diversity, predictability, and coherence of the generated text.\n","\n","## LLM Generation Pipeline\n","\n","Let's understand where sampling occurs in the Transformer architecture.\n","\n","![LLM Generation](llm_generation_pipeline.png)\n","\n","The general flow is:\n","\n","![LLm Generation Flow](llm_generation_flow.png)\n","\n","## Logits\n","\n","The Transformer outputs a vector of raw scores called logits.\n","\n","Suppose the vocabulary has 50,000 tokens.\n","\n","The model's output for the next token may be:\n","\n","$$\n","z = [2.1,\\ 0.8,\\ 4.5,\\ -1.2,\\ ...]\n","$$\n","\n","These logits are not probabilities.\n","\n","To convert them into probabilities, we use the softmax function.\n","\n","$$\n","P(x_i)=\\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_{j=1}^{V}e^{z_j}}\n","$$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $z_i$ = logit of token $i$\n","- $V$ = vocabulary size\n","- $P(x_i)$ = probability of token $i$\n","\n","The sampling algorithm operates on these probabilities, or sometimes directly on logits after applying transformations."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":4,"id":"807a9bfc","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.692368Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.691921Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.697347Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.696238Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017774,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.699425+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.681651+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["# from openai import OpenAI\n","\n","# client = OpenAI(\n","#     base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\",\n","#     api_key=\"my-secret-key\"\n","# )\n","\n","# response = client.chat.completions.create(\n","#     model=\"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\",\n","#     messages=[\n","#         {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain artificial intelligence\"}\n","#     ],\n","#     logprobs=True,\n","#     top_logprobs=5,\n","#     max_tokens=100\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response.choices[0].message.content)\n","\n","# # Inspect token probabilities\n","# for token in response.choices[0].logprobs.content:\n","#     print(\"Token:\", token.token)\n","#     print(\"Logprob:\", token.logprob)\n","\n","#     for alternative in token.top_logprobs:\n","#         print(alternative.token, alternative.logprob)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"f03a6a19","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.007963,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.715756+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.707793+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Temperature\n","\n","Temperature is one of the most commonly used LLM sampling parameters. It controls how sharply or evenly the model's probability distribution is spread across possible tokens.\n","\n","Temperature is a parameter applied to logits before the softmax function.\n","\n","$$\n","P_i = \\frac{e^{z_i/T}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j/T}}\n","$$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- (z_i) = original logit\n","- (T) = temperature\n","- (P_i) = adjusted probability\n","\n","**How temperature works**\n","\n","Suppose the model predicts these logits:\n","\n","| Token | Logit |\n","| ----- | ----- |\n","| cat   | 3.0   |\n","| dog   | 2.0   |\n","| bird  | 1.0   |\n","\n","Let's examine different temperatures.\n","\n","Creating chart\n","\n","- Temperature = 0.1\n","    - The highest probability token becomes extremely dominant.\n","    - The model is almost certain to choose `cat`.\n","- Temperature = 0.5\n","    - The distribution becomes sharper than the original.\n","    - `cat` is very likely, while `dog` remains a possibility.\n","- Temperature = 1.0**\n","    - The original softmax probability distribution remains unchanged.\n","- Temperature = 2.0**\n","    - The distribution becomes flatter.\n","    - `dog` and `bird` become more likely relative to `cat`.\n","\n","### Important temperature behavior\n","\n","| Temperature  | Effect                            |\n","| ------------ | --------------------------------- |\n","| T → 0        | Approaches greedy selection       |\n","| T < 1        | More deterministic                |\n","| T = 1        | Original probability distribution |\n","| T > 1        | More diverse                      |\n","| T → infinity | Distribution approaches uniform   |\n","\n","A technical detail: temperature 0 is often treated as greedy decoding by inference libraries, but the formula itself divides by temperature and is undefined at exactly zero.\n","\n","**When to use temperature**\n","\n","| Application      | Suggested starting point |\n","| ---------------- | ------------------------ |\n","| Code generation  | 0–0.3                    |\n","| Factual Q&A      | 0.1–0.5                  |\n","| RAG answers      | 0–0.3                    |\n","| General chatbot  | 0.5–0.8                  |\n","| Creative writing | 0.8–1.2                  |\n","| Brainstorming    | 1.0–1.5                  |\n","\n","These are illustrative starting points, not universal optimal values. Model behavior differs.\n","\n","**Interview point:** Temperature does not add new knowledge to the model. It changes how likely the model is to select tokens already represented in its output distribution."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":5,"id":"6a526fea","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.734367Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.733896Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.739491Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.738091Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017655,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.741763+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.724108+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["# import torch\n","# import torch.nn.functional as F\n","\n","# def temperature_sampling(logits, temperature=1.0):\n","\n","#     # Scale logits\n","#     scaled_logits = logits / temperature\n","\n","#     # Convert logits into probabilities\n","#     probabilities = F.softmax(scaled_logits, dim=-1)\n","\n","#     # Randomly sample a token\n","#     next_token = torch.multinomial(\n","#         probabilities,\n","#         num_samples=1\n","#     )\n","\n","#     return next_token, probabilities\n","\n","\n","# # Example logits\n","# logits = torch.tensor([2.0, 1.0, 0.1])\n","\n","# token, probs = temperature_sampling(\n","#     logits,\n","#     temperature=0.7\n","# )\n","\n","# print(\"Probabilities:\", probs)\n","# print(\"Selected token:\", token)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":6,"id":"1e968f09","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.760394Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.759963Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.764867Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.763958Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.016904,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.766997+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.750093+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["# from groq import Groq\n","\n","# client = Groq()\n","\n","# response = client.chat.completions.create(\n","#     model=\"llama-3.3-70b-versatile\",\n","#     messages=[\n","#         {\n","#             \"role\": \"user\",\n","#             \"content\": \"Explain artificial intelligence\"\n","#         }\n","#     ],\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","#     max_tokens=200\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response.choices[0].message.content)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"04d528bb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008485,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.783826+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.775341+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Greedy Decoding\n","\n","Greedy decoding is the simplest token-selection strategy.\n","\n","It selects the token with the highest probability at every generation step.\n","\n","$$\n","x_t = \\arg\\max_{x} P(x \\mid x_{<t})\n","$$\n","\n","Here:\n","\n","- $x_t$ is the next token.\n","- $x_{<t}$ is the previously generated context.\n","- $\\arg\\max$ means select the token with the highest probability.\n","\n","Prompt:\n","\n","> The capital of France is\n","\n","The model predicts:\n","\n","| Token  | Probability |\n","| ------ | ----------- |\n","| Paris  | 85%         |\n","| Lyon   | 8%          |\n","| London | 5%          |\n","| Berlin | 2%          |\n","\n","Greedy decoding selects:\n","\n","`Paris`\n","\n","Now suppose the next-token probabilities are:\n","\n","| Token | Probability |\n","| ----- | ----------- |\n","| .     | 60%         |\n","| ,     | 20%         |\n","| and   | 15%         |\n","| !     | 5%          |\n","\n","It selects:\n","\n","`.`\n","\n","Generated response:\n","\n","> The capital of France is Paris.\n","\n","**Advantages**\n","\n","- Fast and simple.\n","- Reproducible.\n","- Good for predictable outputs.\n","- No random sampling required.\n","\n","**Disadvantages**\n","\n","- Can generate repetitive text.\n","- May produce dull or generic answers.\n","- Can get stuck in loops.\n","- Locally optimal choices may not produce the globally best sequence.\n","\n","**When to use it**\n","\n","- Basic classification-style generation.\n","- Predictable, short outputs.\n","- Constrained generation where diversity is undesirable.\n","\n","Important: Greedy decoding is not the same as beam search. Beam search explores multiple candidate sequences, whereas greedy decoding only follows the single highest-probability token at each step.\n","\n","**Compare decoding strategies**\n","\n","| Temperature | Behavior |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| 0.0 | Greedy-like selection |\n","| 0.2 | Very deterministic |\n","| 0.5 | More focused |\n","| 0.7 | Balanced creativity |\n","| 1.0 | Original distribution |\n","| 1.5 | More random |\n","| 2.0 | Highly random |\n","\n","Note: Temperature 0 is commonly treated as greedy decoding by inference APIs. The mathematical temperature formula itself is undefined at exactly zero.\n","\n","## Multinomial sampling (random sampling)**\n","\n","Multinomial sampling selects a token randomly according to the probability distribution. It is sometimes called categorical sampling when selecting a single token.\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","The model produces:\n","\n","| Token  | Probability |\n","| ------ | ----------- |\n","| apple  | 50%         |\n","| banana | 30%         |\n","| orange | 20%         |\n","\n","Imagine a random number (r) between 0 and 1.\n","\n","Construct cumulative probability intervals:\n","\n","| Token  | Interval  |\n","| ------ | --------- |\n","| apple  | 0.00–0.50 |\n","| banana | 0.50–0.80 |\n","| orange | 0.80–1.00 |\n","\n","If:\n","\n","- (r=0.23): choose apple.\n","- (r=0.67): choose banana.\n","- (r=0.91): choose orange.\n","\n","The token is not necessarily the highest-probability one. It is selected based on its probability.\n","\n","**Why this matters**\n","\n","Suppose the prompt is:\n","\n","> Write a creative opening sentence about a forest.\n","\n","If the model always chooses the highest-probability token, many generations may be similar.\n","\n","With random sampling, the model may generate different valid openings.\n","\n","Multinomial sampling is a fundamental mechanism behind many popular sampling approaches, including top-k and top-p sampling.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":7,"id":"7ad5e458","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.803558Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.803148Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.808845Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.807705Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017597,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.810891+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.793294+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# from groq import Groq\n","\n","# client = Groq()\n","\n","# response = client.chat.completions.create(\n","#     model=\"qwen/qwen3.8-27b\",\n","\n","#     messages=[\n","#         {\n","#             \"role\": \"user\",\n","#             \"content\": [\n","#                 {\n","#                     \"type\": \"text\",\n","#                     \"text\": \"Describe this image in detail.\"\n","#                 },\n","#                 {\n","#                     \"type\": \"image_url\",\n","#                     \"image_url\": {\n","#                         \"url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/LPU-v1-die.jpg\"\n","#                     }\n","#                 }\n","#             ]\n","#         }\n","#     ],\n","\n","#     # Generation sampling settings\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","#     top_p=0.9,\n","#     max_completion_tokens=500\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response.choices[0].message.content)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"bfceb6ef","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008017,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.82728+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.819263+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Top-k sampling\n","\n","Limits candidate tokens\n","\n","Top-k sampling restricts the next-token choices to the k most probable tokens.\n","\n","All other tokens are removed from the distribution."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":8,"id":"8bd66e49","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.84595Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.844907Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.850018Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.84891Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.016788,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.852087+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.835299+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# from groq import Groq\n","\n","# client = Groq()\n","\n","# response = client.chat.completions.create(\n","#     model=\"qwen/qwen3.8-27b\",\n","#     messages=[\n","#         {\n","#             \"role\": \"user\",\n","#             \"content\": \"Explain artificial intelligence\"\n","#         }\n","#     ],\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","#     top_p=0.9,\n","#     max_completion_tokens=200\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response.choices[0].message.content)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"50794edc","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008528,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.869284+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.860756+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**How it works**\n","\n","Suppose the LLM predicts:\n","\n","| Token  | Original probability |\n","| ------ | -------------------- |\n","| cat    | 40%                  |\n","| dog    | 25%                  |\n","| bird   | 15%                  |\n","| fish   | 10%                  |\n","| rabbit | 5%                   |\n","| tiger  | 3%                   |\n","| lion   | 2%                   |\n","\n","Let's set:\n","\n","$$\n","k=3\n","$$\n","\n","The model keeps the top three tokens:\n","\n","- cat\n","- dog\n","- bird\n","\n","The rest are eliminated.\n","\n","Then the remaining probabilities are renormalized.\n","\n","**Probability renormalization**\n","\n","The retained probabilities sum to:\n","\n","$$\n","40%+25%+15%=80%\n","$$\n","\n","The new probabilities become:\n","\n","$$\n","P(\\text{cat})=\\frac{40}{80}=50%\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","P(\\text{dog})=\\frac{25}{80}=31.25%\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","P(\\text{bird})=\\frac{15}{80}=18.75%\n","$$\n","\n","Creating chart\n","\n","Now random sampling chooses among these three tokens.\n","\n","**What does k control?**\n","\n","| k            | Behavior                         |\n","| ------------ | -------------------------------- |\n","| 1            | Equivalent to greedy selection   |\n","| 3            | Only three likely tokens         |\n","| 10           | More diversity                   |\n","| 50           | Much larger candidate pool       |\n","| Very large k | Approaches unrestricted sampling |\n","\n","**Advantages**\n","\n","- Removes unlikely tokens.\n","- Prevents some bizarre continuations.\n","- Gives direct control over the candidate pool.\n","\n","**Disadvantages**\n","\n","- Fixed candidate size.\n","- A suitable k for one context might be unsuitable for another.\n","- Can exclude reasonable tokens when the distribution is diffuse.\n","\n","For example, when the model predicts a very obvious next token, top-k may retain several near-equivalent options. In a creative context, the distribution may have a much broader set of reasonable alternatives.\n","\n","## Top-p sampling (Nucleus sampling)\n","\n","Dynamic candidate pool\n","\n","Top-p sampling is one of the most popular sampling strategies for modern LLMs.\n","\n","Unlike top-k, it does not use a fixed number of candidate tokens.\n","\n","Instead, it selects the smallest set of the highest-probability tokens whose cumulative probability reaches or exceeds a threshold (p).\n","\n","This is called the nucleus.\n","\n","**Formula**\n","\n","First, sort tokens by probability:\n","\n","$$\n","P(x_1)\\ge P(x_2)\\ge ...\\ge P(x_n)\n","$$\n","\n","Then find the smallest set (S) satisfying:\n","\n","$$\n","\\sum\\_{x_i\\in S}P(x_i)\\ge p\n","$$\n","\n","After removing other tokens, renormalize the retained distribution.\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose the model produces:\n","\n","| Token  | Probability | Cumulative probability |\n","| ------ | ----------- | ---------------------- |\n","| cat    | 40%         | 40%                    |\n","| dog    | 25%         | 65%                    |\n","| bird   | 15%         | 80%                    |\n","| fish   | 10%         | 90%                    |\n","| rabbit | 5%          | 95%                    |\n","| tiger  | 3%          | 98%                    |\n","| lion   | 2%          | 100%                   |\n","\n","Set:\n","\n","$$\n","p=0.90\n","$$\n","\n","The nucleus contains:\n","\n","- cat\n","- dog\n","- bird\n","- fish\n","\n","Because their cumulative probability reaches 90%.\n","\n","The model removes rabbit, tiger, and lion.\n","\n","The new probabilities are:\n","\n","| Token | New probability |\n","| ----- | --------------- |\n","| cat   | 44.44%          |\n","| dog   | 27.78%          |\n","| bird  | 16.67%          |\n","| fish  | 11.11%          |\n","\n","**Why top-p is different from top-k**\n","\n","Consider two different situations.\n","\n","**Sharp distribution**\n","\n","![Sharp Distribution](sharp_distribution.png)\n","\n","A few tokens carry most of the probability mass.\n","\n","**Flat distribution**\n","\n","![Flat Distribution](flat_distribution.png)\n","\n","Probability is spread across many tokens.\n","\n","With top-p = 0.9:\n","\n","- Sharp distribution: perhaps only 2 or 3 tokens are retained.\n","- Flat distribution: perhaps 20 or 50 tokens are retained.\n","\n","The number of retained tokens adapts to the distribution."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":9,"id":"518fd884","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.890562Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.889484Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.894433Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.89341Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019066,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.896492+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.877426+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# from groq import Groq\n","\n","# client = Groq()\n","\n","# response = client.chat.completions.create(\n","#     model=\"llama-3.3-70b-versatile\",\n","\n","#     messages=[\n","#         {\n","#             \"role\": \"user\",\n","#             \"content\": \"Explain artificial intelligence\"\n","#         }\n","#     ],\n","\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","#     top_p=0.9,\n","#     max_tokens=200\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response.choices[0].message.content)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ac58bb60","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008659,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.913468+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.904809+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Top-p values**\n","\n","| p    | Behavior                       |\n","| ---- | ------------------------------ |\n","| 0.1  | Very restrictive               |\n","| 0.5  | Restrictive                    |\n","| 0.8  | Moderate                       |\n","| 0.9  | Common setting                 |\n","| 0.95 | More diverse                   |\n","| 1.0  | No probability-mass truncation |\n","\n","**Interview answer:** Top-k uses a fixed number of candidates, whereas top-p dynamically selects a number of candidates based on cumulative probability mass.\n","\n","## Min-p sampling\n","\n","Relative probability filtering\n","\n","Min-p is another modern sampling strategy.\n","\n","Instead of keeping a fixed number of tokens or a fixed cumulative probability mass, min-p filters tokens based on their probability relative to the highest-probability token.\n","\n","**Formula**\n","\n","$$\n","P(x_i)\\ge p\\_{\\min}\\cdot P\\_{\\max}\n","$$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $P(x_i)$ = probability of a candidate token.\n","- $P\\_{\\max}$ = highest token probability.\n","- $p\\_{\\min}$ = min-p threshold.\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose:\n","\n","| Token  | Probability |\n","| ------ | ----------- |\n","| cat    | 50%         |\n","| dog    | 25%         |\n","| bird   | 10%         |\n","| fish   | 5%          |\n","| rabbit | 2%          |\n","\n","Set:\n","\n","$$\n","p\\_{\\min}=0.2\n","$$\n","\n","The minimum acceptable probability is:\n","\n","$$\n","0.2 \\times 50%=10%\n","$$\n","\n","Therefore:\n","\n","- cat: retained\n","- dog: retained\n","- bird: retained\n","- fish: removed\n","- rabbit: removed\n","\n","The remaining tokens are then renormalized."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":10,"id":"8d3f8700","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.932033Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.931508Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.936703Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.935552Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017117,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.938944+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.921827+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# import torch\n","# from transformers import (\n","#     AutoTokenizer,\n","#     AutoModelForCausalLM\n","# )\n","\n","# # Load model\n","# model_name = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"\n","\n","# tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\n","\n","# model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n","#     model_name,\n","#     torch_dtype=\"auto\",\n","#     device_map=\"auto\"\n","# )\n","\n","# # Input prompt\n","# prompt = \"Explain artificial intelligence\"\n","\n","# inputs = tokenizer(\n","#     prompt,\n","#     return_tensors=\"pt\"\n","# ).to(model.device)\n","\n","# # Generate with Min-P sampling\n","# outputs = model.generate(\n","#     **inputs,\n","\n","#     max_new_tokens=200,\n","\n","#     do_sample=True,\n","\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","\n","#     min_p=0.1,\n","\n","#     # Disable other sampling filters\n","#     top_k=0,\n","#     top_p=1.0\n","# )\n","\n","# # Decode output\n","# response = tokenizer.decode(\n","#     outputs[0],\n","#     skip_special_tokens=True\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"b0f3b84e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008817,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.956743+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.947926+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Min-p versus top-p**\n","\n","| Feature                  | Top-p                  | Min-p                                                |\n","| ------------------------ | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Filtering basis          | Cumulative probability | Relative to highest probability                      |\n","| Candidate count          | Dynamic                | Dynamic                                              |\n","| Depends on highest token | Indirectly             | Directly                                             |\n","| Common use               | General generation     | Modern LLM inference, especially creative generation |\n","\n","Min-p can adapt well to both confident and uncertain predictions. It is often used in inference systems alongside temperature.\n","\n","## Typical sampling\n","\n","Entropy-based selection\n","\n","Typical sampling is a more advanced method.\n","\n","Its goal is to select tokens that are statistically typical according to the model's probability distribution.\n","\n","The central idea:\n","\n","A token can be very probable, but it may not be representative of the distribution's typical information content.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":11,"id":"af8d7d6e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.976229Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.975419Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.980929Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.979884Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017403,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.983046+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.965643+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["\n","# import torch\n","# from transformers import (\n","#     AutoTokenizer,\n","#     AutoModelForCausalLM\n","# )\n","\n","# model_name = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"\n","\n","# tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\n","\n","# model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n","#     model_name,\n","#     torch_dtype=\"auto\",\n","#     device_map=\"auto\"\n","# )\n","\n","# prompt = \"Explain artificial intelligence\"\n","\n","# inputs = tokenizer(\n","#     prompt,\n","#     return_tensors=\"pt\"\n","# ).to(model.device)\n","\n","# outputs = model.generate(\n","#     **inputs,\n","#     max_new_tokens=200,\n","#     do_sample=True,\n","#     temperature=0.7,\n","\n","#     # Typical sampling\n","#     typical_p=0.95,\n","\n","#     # Disable other sampling filters\n","#     top_k=0,\n","#     top_p=1.0\n","# )\n","\n","# response = tokenizer.decode(\n","#     outputs[0],\n","#     skip_special_tokens=True\n","# )\n","\n","# print(response)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"11389d03","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.008608,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.000179+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:05.991571+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Information content**\n","\n","The information content of a token is:\n","\n","$$\n","I(x)=-\\log P(x)\n","$$\n","\n","The entropy of a distribution is:\n","\n","$$\n","H(P)=-\\sum_x P(x)\\log P(x)\n","$$\n","\n","Typical sampling considers how close a token's information content is to the distribution's entropy.\n","\n","A simplified typicality measure is:\n","\n","$$\n","d(x)=|-\\log P(x)-H(P)|\n","$$\n","\n","Tokens with smaller (d(x)) are more typical.\n","\n","Typical sampling sorts tokens by this measure and retains a set based on a probability-mass parameter, often called `typical_p`.\n","\n","**Why it exists**\n","\n","Imagine the model predicts:\n","\n","- A token that is overwhelmingly probable but very predictable.\n","- Several tokens with moderate probabilities and information content closer to the distribution's entropy.\n","\n","Typical sampling can favor tokens that are representative of the model's distribution rather than simply those with the highest probability.\n","\n","It attempts to balance coherence and diversity.\n","\n","**When it is useful**\n","\n","- Natural language generation.\n","- Reducing repetitive or overly predictable outputs.\n","- Experimental text generation.\n","\n","Typical sampling is less commonly exposed in mainstream API interfaces than temperature and top-p.\n","\n","## Epsilon sampling and Eta sampling\n","\n","These are advanced probability-threshold filtering methods.\n","\n","### Epsilon sampling\n","\n","Epsilon sampling removes tokens below a fixed probability threshold:\n","\n","$$\n","P(x_i)<\\epsilon\n","$$\n","\n","Those tokens are eliminated.\n","\n","For example, if:\n","\n","$$\n","\\epsilon=0.01\n","$$\n","\n","then all tokens with probability less than 1% are removed.\n","\n","Unlike top-k, it doesn't guarantee a specific candidate count.\n","\n","Unlike top-p, it doesn't guarantee that a particular cumulative mass is retained."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"9b7f80c8","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","papermill":{"duration":0.008323,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.017448+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.009125+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Tracing\n","\n","Tracing is a technique used to monitor and record the sequence of events that occur while a system, application, or program is running.\n","\n","It helps developers understand:\n","\n","- How a program executes.\n","- Which functions or methods are called.\n","- How data moves between different components.\n","- Where errors occur.\n","- How much time each operation takes.\n","- Why a system behaves unexpectedly.\n","\n","**How Does Tracing Work?**\n","\n","Tracing generally follows these steps:\n","\n","1. Start an operation: A user initiates an operation.\n","2. Generate a trace ID: A unique identifier is assigned to the operation.\n","3. Record events: The system records important activities.\n","4. Collect timestamps: Each operation gets a start time and end time.\n","5. Track execution: The system follows the operation through different components.\n","6. Analyze results: Developers examine the recorded information to identify errors, delays, or unexpected behavior.\n","\n","## Problem Statement\n","\n","Imagine that you build a RAG chatbot. The chatbot uses company PDF documents to answer questions about company policies.\n","\n","A user asks:\n","\n","> What is the refund policy of this company?\n","\n","The chatbot answers:\n","\n","> The company provides a 90-day refund policy.\n","\n","However, the actual company document says:\n","\n","> Customers can request a refund within 30 days of purchase.\n","\n","The chatbot has generated an incorrect answer.\n","\n","Without tracing, you may have to manually investigate:\n","\n","- What query was sent to database?\n","- Which document chunks were retrieved?\n","- Were the retrieved chunks relevant?\n","- Was the prompt correctly constructed?\n","- What exactly was sent to LLM?\n","- What did LLM return?\n","- How long did each operation take?\n","- Was the generated answer grounded in the retrieved documents?\n","\n","Tracing helps you inspect these operations through traces.\n","\n","The main idea:\n","\n","> Tracing makes the internal behavior of your AI application observable.\n","\n","\n","**Advantages of Tracing**\n","\n","1. **Easy debugging:** Helps identify where errors occur.\n","2. **Performance optimization:** Identifies slow operations.\n","3. **Better visibility:** Shows how different components interact.\n","4. **Problem identification:** Helps locate the source of failures.\n","5. **Improved reliability:** Helps developers understand and resolve system problems.\n","6. **Dependency analysis:** Shows relationships between components.\n","\n","**Disadvantages of Tracing**\n","\n","- It can generate large amounts of data.\n","- Collecting and storing traces can increase costs.\n","- Instrumentation requires additional development effort.\n","- Improper configuration can create performance overhead.\n","- Sensitive information must be protected from appearing in trace data.\n","\n","\n","## Components\n","\n","In modern software systems, tracing commonly consists of several components.\n","\n","This hierarchical structure is called a **trace tree**.\n","\n","**Important Tracing Terminology**\n","\n","| Term | Meaning |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| Trace | Complete execution record of a request |\n","| Run | One execution of a component or operation |\n","| Parent run | Run that contains or invokes another run |\n","| Child run | Nested execution step |\n","| Project | Grouping of traces |\n","| Thread | Collection of related conversational runs |\n","| Tags | Labels used to filter traces |\n","| Metadata | Additional structured information |\n","| Feedback | Score or annotation associated with a run |\n","\n","A useful mental model:\n","\n","- Run = One operation.\n","- Trace = The complete request execution.\n","- Thread = Related turns of a conversation.\n","- Project = Collection of executions for an application or environment.\n","\n","### Trace\n","\n","A trace represents the complete journey of a single request through a system.\n","\n","In our RAG chatbot, when a user asks about the refund policy, the entire execution process can be represented by one trace.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```text\n","Trace ID: ABC123\n","\n","User Request\n","     |\n","     v\n","FastAPI Endpoint\n","     |\n","     v\n","Question Processing\n","     |\n","     v\n","Query Embedding\n","     |\n","     v\n","Database Retrieval\n","     |\n","     v\n","Prompt Construction\n","     |\n","     v\n","    LLM\n","     |\n","     v\n","Final Response\n","```\n","\n","Each trace has a unique Trace ID that helps identify and correlate its activities.\n","\n","### Run\n","\n","A run represents one execution of a component or operation.\n","\n","In our RAG chatbot, examples include:\n","\n","- A complete RAG pipeline execution.\n","- A retrieval operation.\n","- A database similarity search.\n","- A prompt formatting operation.\n","- A LLM invocation.\n","- A document-processing function.\n","\n","A trace generally consists of a root run and its child runs.\n","\n","Consider the following trace tree:\n","\n","text\n","\n","```text\n","Trace ID: ABC123\n","\n","RAG Chatbot (Root Run)\n","    |\n","    |--- Question Processing\n","    |\n","    |--- Document Retrieval\n","    |       |\n","    |       |--- Query Embedding\n","    |       |\n","    |       |--- FAISS Similarity Search\n","    |\n","    |--- Prompt Construction\n","    |\n","    |--- LLM\n","    |\n","    |--- Final Response\n","```\n","\n","Each run represents an individual execution step.\n","\n","**Example of Runs**\n","\n","| Run | Input | Output |\n","| --- | --- | --- |\n","| Root run | User question | Final chatbot answer |\n","| Question processing | User question | Processed question |\n","| Query embedding | Processed question | Query vector |\n","| Database retrieval | Query vector | Retrieved document chunks |\n","| Prompt construction | Question + context | Formatted prompt |\n","| LLM | Prompt/messages | Generated answer |\n","| Final response | Generated answer | API response |\n","\n","**Components of a Run**\n","\n","A run commonly contains:\n","\n","- Inputs.\n","- Outputs.\n","- Start time.\n","- End time.\n","- Duration.\n","- Errors.\n","- Metadata.\n","- Tags.\n","- Parent-child relationships.\n","- Feedback and evaluation results.\n","\n","Some runs can also include token usage and model cost.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```text\n","Run: Gemini LLM\n","\n","Input:\n","    Question: What is the refund policy?\n","    Context: Customers can request a refund within 30 days.\n","\n","Output:\n","    The company provides a 90-day refund policy.\n","\n","Start Time: 10:30:01\n","End Time:   10:30:03\n","Duration:   2 seconds\n","\n","Status: Success\n","```\n","\n","Although the LLM execution succeeded technically, its answer was factually incorrect.\n","\n","This is why tracing is useful, it helps distinguish execution failures from answer-quality problems.\n","\n","## Types of Tracing\n","\n","Tracing can be classified into several types depending on its purpose.\n","\n","| Type | Description | RAG Chatbot Example |\n","| --- | --- | --- |\n","| Distributed tracing | Tracks requests across multiple services | Following a request across FastAPI, embedding service, and model API |\n","| Application tracing | Tracks application execution | Following the RAG pipeline |\n","| Network tracing | Monitors network communication | Investigating communication with the Gemini API |\n","| Database tracing | Tracks database queries and operations | Monitoring metadata database queries |\n","| Function tracing | Records function calls and execution | Tracking the retrieval function |\n","| System tracing | Monitors operating system activities | Investigating CPU or system-level operations |\n","| Performance tracing | Measures execution time and bottlenecks | Finding slow retrieval or LLM operations |\n","\n","## Tracing vs Logging vs Monitoring\n","\n","These three concepts are related but have different purposes.\n","\n","| Feature | Tracing | Logging | Monitoring |\n","| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n","| Main purpose | Follow request execution | Record events | Observe system health |\n","| Focus | Request journey | Individual messages and events | Metrics and system status |\n","| Information | Spans and relationships | Text and structured events | CPU, memory, latency |\n","| Main use | Find where a request fails or slows down | Understand what happened | Detect system problems |\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose our RAG chatbot takes 5 seconds to answer a question.\n","\n","- **Monitoring:** Tells us that the chatbot's average response time has increased to 5 seconds.\n","- **Logging:** Shows that the Gemini API returned a timeout error for a particular request.\n","- **Tracing:** Shows that FAISS retrieval took 30 ms, prompt construction took 10 ms, and Gemini took 4.5 seconds.\n","\n","Together, these technologies provide a better understanding of system behavior.\n","\n","## Distributed Tracing\n","\n","Consider our RAG chatbot architecture:\n","\n","- FastAPI: Receives user requests.\n","- Embedding Model: Converts questions into vectors.\n","- Vectorr Database: Retrieves relevant document chunks.\n","- LLM: Generates answers using retrieved context.\n","\n","A user asks:\n","\n","> \"What is the refund policy of this company?\"\n","\n","The request travels through the application.\n","\n","The trace might look like this:\n","\n","```text\n","Trace ID: ABC123\n","\n","Request: What is the refund policy?\n","\n","[FastAPI]                 20 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [Question Processing]   10 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [Embedding Model]       50 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [FAISS Retrieval]       30 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [Prompt Construction]   10 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [Gemini API]          1500 ms\n","    |\n","    |---- [Response Processing]   10 ms\n","```\n","\n","By examining this trace, developers can understand:\n","\n","- Which component received the request.\n","- Which operation executed next.\n","- How long each operation took.\n","- Which document chunks were retrieved.\n","- Whether an operation returned an error.\n","- Where performance problems occurred.\n","\n","## Tools\n","\n","Some popular tracing tools and technologies include:\n","\n","**OpenTelemetry**\n","\n","- An open-source framework for collecting traces, metrics, and logs.\n","- Supports instrumentation across different programming languages and frameworks.\n","- [Official website](<https://opentelemetry.io/>)\n","\n","**Jaeger**\n","\n","- Used to monitor and troubleshoot distributed applications.\n","- Provides trace visualization and analysis.\n","- [Official website](<https://www.jaegertracing.io/>)\n","\n","**Grafana Tempo**\n","\n","- A distributed tracing backend.\n","- Stores and queries trace data.\n","- Integrates with Grafana.\n","- [Official website](<https://grafana.com/oss/tempo/>)\n","\n","**Zipkin**\n","\n","- Helps collect and visualize timing data from distributed systems.\n","- [Official website](<https://zipkin.io/>)\n","\n","**LangSmith**\n","\n","- Designed for observing, debugging, evaluating, and monitoring LLM applications.\n","- Provides detailed traces of AI application executions.\n","- [Official website](<https://smith.langchain.com/>)\n","\n","## LangSmith vs LangChain vs LangGraph vs LangChain Core\n","\n","These tools have different responsibilities in the LangChain ecosystem.\n","\n","| Tool | Main Responsibility |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| LangChain | Build LLM applications using components, integrations, and chains |\n","| LangGraph | Build stateful, graph-based AI agents and workflows |\n","| LangChain Core | Provides fundamental abstractions, runnable interfaces, messages, prompts, etc. |\n","| LangSmith | Trace, debug, evaluate, monitor, and improve AI applications |\n","\n","Imagine you are building our RAG chatbot.\n","\n","- **LangChain** provides components for document retrieval, prompt construction, and LLM integration.\n","- **LangGraph** can control a stateful RAG or agent workflow.\n","- **LangChain Core** provides the fundamental interfaces and abstractions.\n","- **LangSmith** lets you inspect and evaluate the execution.\n","\n","**Important:** LangSmith does not require LangChain or LangGraph.\n","\n","You can use it with:\n","\n","- OpenAI API directly.\n","- Gemini API directly.\n","- Anthropic API.\n","- FastAPI applications.\n","- Custom Python applications.\n","- Other agent frameworks.\n","\n","It is designed to support framework-independent observability through its SDK and tracing integrations.\n","\n","## Importance of Tracing\n","\n","Imagine our RAG chatbot gives the following incorrect answer:\n","\n","User question:\n","\n","> What is the refund policy?\n","\n","Actual document:\n","\n","> Customers can request a refund within 30 days of purchase.\n","\n","Chatbot answer:\n","\n","> The company provides a 90-day refund policy.\n","\n","Without tracing, you may not know where the problem occurred. With tracing, you can inspect the complete execution.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```text\n","Trace ID: ABC123\n","\n","1. User Question\n","   |\n","   | Input:\n","   | What is the refund policy?\n","   |\n","   v\n","\n","2. FAISS Retrieval\n","   |\n","   | Retrieved Context:\n","   | No relevant refund policy found.\n","   |\n","   v\n","\n","3. Prompt Construction\n","   |\n","   | Question + Retrieved Context\n","   |\n","   v\n","\n","4. Gemini LLM\n","   |\n","   | Generated Answer:\n","   | The company provides a 90-day refund policy.\n","   |\n","   v\n","\n","5. Final Response\n","   |\n","   | Returned to User\n","```\n","\n","By examining the trace, you discover that the retrieval step did not provide the correct refund policy.\n","\n","The problem may be related to document chunking, embedding quality, similarity search, or retrieval configuration.\n","\n","Instead of blindly changing the LLM, you can investigate the actual source of the problem.\n","\n","## LangSmith\n","\n","Go to [LangSmith Platform](<https://smith.langchain.com/>).\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","1. Create an account.\n","2. Create or select a workspace.\n","3. Generate an API key.\n","4. Create a project.\n","5. Configure your application.\n","\n","Install the package:\n","\n","```bash\n","pip install -U langsmith\n","```\n","\n","**Environment Configuration**\n","\n","Create a `.env` file:\n","\n","```env\n","LANGSMITH_TRACING=true\n","LANGSMITH_API_KEY=ls_your_api_key\n","LANGSMITH_PROJECT=rag-development\n","```\n","\n","Optional endpoint:\n","\n","```env\n","LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT=https://api.smith.langchain.com\n","```\n","\n","For other regions, use the endpoint specified by your workspace.\n","\n","**Environment Variables**\n","\n","| Environment Variable | Purpose |\n","| --- | --- |\n","| `LANGSMITH_TRACING` | Enables tracing |\n","| `LANGSMITH_API_KEY` | Authenticates the SDK |\n","| `LANGSMITH_PROJECT` | Determines the project where traces are grouped |\n","| `LANGSMITH_ENDPOINT` | Specifies the LangSmith API endpoint |\n","\n","The SDK can automatically trace supported LangChain executions when tracing is enabled. Automatic tracing records supported LangChain operations. It does not necessarily record every internal operation of Databse, Application, or external services. Additional instrumentation may be required for those operations, only LLM operation has been traced automatically.\n","\n","Enabled doesn't mean, placed the variable in `.env`, you must load it to the `os` to get the automatic tracing."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":12,"id":"859875a9","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.036081Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.034879Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.113911Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.112788Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.090568,"end_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.116165+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-20T12:13:06.025597+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["LangSmith tracing configured!\n"]}],"source":["import os\n","from kaggle_se